WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.978
Embedded systems are not just smaller
versions of server applications.

2
00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:07.993
They interact with sensors, actuators,
radios, buses, storage, debug hardware,

3
00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:11.038
boot chains, update mechanisms, and
physical environments.

4
00:00:11.118 --> 00:00:14.597
A firmware decision can affect not only
data confidentiality or service

5
00:00:14.677 --> 00:00:17.243
availability, but also device behavior in
the field.

6
00:00:17.323 --> 00:00:21.144
Rust provides meaningful security
advantages through ownership, borrowing,

7
00:00:21.224 --> 00:00:23.334
type safety, and memory safety in safe
code.

8
00:00:23.414 --> 00:00:27.063
Those advantages are important in firmware
because many embedded failures

9
00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:30.565
historically come from memory corruption,
unclear buffer ownership,

10
00:00:30.645 --> 00:00:32.355
and fragile low-level interfaces.

11
00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:35.344
But Rust does not remove the need for
hardware-aware design.

12
00:00:35.424 --> 00:00:38.104
Firmware can still fail through weak input
validation,

13
00:00:38.184 --> 00:00:41.947
unsafe hardware assumptions, exposed debug
paths, fragile update behavior,

14
00:00:42.027 --> 00:00:45.221
secret leakage, unbounded resource use,
poor dependency review,

15
00:00:45.301 --> 00:00:47.981
or release processes that cannot be
repeated reliably.

16
00:00:48.061 --> 00:00:51.710
These are engineering and lifecycle
problems, not only language problems.

17
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.699
Embedded Rust security means treating
hardware, firmware,

18
00:00:54.779 --> 00:00:58.884
build systems, manufacturing, update
channels, and field operations as one system.

19
00:00:58.964 --> 00:01:02.671
Assumptions about memory, timing, storage,
debug access, device identity,

20
00:01:02.751 --> 00:01:06.001
and failure behavior should be written
down, tested, and reviewed.

21
00:01:06.081 --> 00:01:08.990
The defensive goal is safe, bounded,
field-ready behavior.

22
00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.206
Code should make ownership clear, keep
unsafe boundaries small,

23
00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:15.936
reject invalid input predictably, protect
secrets, constrain resources,

24
00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:19.380
and support maintenance without relying on
hidden tribal knowledge.
